findings.description:
Line 133 admits the cost asymmetry (false brownfield writes wrong artifacts) but leaves "signals and thresholds" to slice design. Slice 261 shipped, so the thresholds exist in code and the architecture doc is stale. Line 73 describes "source volume (a floor, because counting stops at the threshold)", which is an implementation detail with no stated floor.

Also missing:
- how detection treats vendored directories, generated code, `node_modules`, or monorepo siblings (line 79 scopes to a subfolder, but the ignore rules are not stated);
- the cost bound on counting in a very large repo;
- how it behaves for a project whose source is mostly in non-code formats.

The override (line 133, "the safety valve") is in effect the only mitigation. But line 104 says an unadopted brownfield repo gets "one line" with how to opt out. The user sees that line only if they run `cf next`, and `sq onboard` is not guaranteed to surface it.

Slice 271 is cited as the contract in lines 81, 91, 118, 136 and 160, but this document gives only prose:
- the detection report is "JSON" with "facts"; no schema is given, and neither is versioning or error semantics;
- prompt delivery says "with the comprehension doc's path filled in," while line 136 says "slice 271's design settles how the profile's variables are delivered";
- there is no statement of how the missing-guide-prompt error surfaces through the CLI and MCP (line 53 says it is an error, never a fallback);
- no ownership of the monorepo case: whether the CLI command takes a project path or reads the registered project.

The architecture needs at least the shape of this interface, because it is the one thing squadron will code against. Principle line 48 also says `cf init` and `cf next` consume the same function. The first-run logic in `WorkflowNavigator.detectFirstRunContext` (line 59) is not stated to be replaced or reconciled, so there may be two classifiers.
